__d(function(e,a,t,r,n,o,i){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:5,chapter_title:"Lines and Angles",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are complementary angles? Give an example.",back:"Two angles are complementary if their measures sum to exactly 90\xb0. They do NOT need to be adjacent (next to each other). Example: 35\xb0 and 55\xb0 are complementary (35 + 55 = 90). Also: 45\xb0 and 45\xb0, or 20\xb0 and 70\xb0. The complement of any angle x\xb0 = (90 \u2212 x)\xb0.",memory_hook:"COMPLEMENTARY = 90\xb0 = C for Corner (right angle is a corner). C-9: Complementary = 9 tens = 90\xb0."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are supplementary angles? Give an example.",back:"Two angles are supplementary if their measures sum to exactly 180\xb0. They do NOT need to be adjacent. Example: 110\xb0 and 70\xb0 are supplementary (110 + 70 = 180). Also: 90\xb0 and 90\xb0, or 135\xb0 and 45\xb0. The supplement of any angle x\xb0 = (180 \u2212 x)\xb0.",memory_hook:"SUPPLEMENTARY = 180\xb0 = S for Straight line (180\xb0 is a straight angle). S-18: Supplementary = 18 tens = 180\xb0."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a linear pair of angles? What is the linear pair axiom?",back:"A linear pair is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two rays emerge from the same point on a straight line. Their non-common arms together form a straight line (180\xb0). Linear pair axiom: If two angles form a linear pair, they are supplementary \u2014 they sum to 180\xb0. This is stated as an axiom (accepted without proof). Example: If \u2220AOB = 65\xb0 and \u2220AOB forms a linear pair with \u2220BOC, then \u2220BOC = 115\xb0.",memory_hook:"LINEAR = straight LINE. Linear pair makes a LINE = 180\xb0. If you see two angles sitting on a straight line, they're a linear pair and must sum to 180\xb0."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are vertically opposite angles? State their key property.",back:"When two straight lines intersect at a point, four angles are formed. The two pairs of angles that are across from each other (not next to each other) are called vertically opposite angles. Key property: Vertically opposite angles are ALWAYS EQUAL. Example: if two lines intersect and one angle = 70\xb0, then the angle directly opposite = 70\xb0, and the two adjacent angles = 110\xb0 each. The word 'vertically' refers to the vertex, not direction.",memory_hook:"VERTICALLY OPPOSITE = V-O = Equal. Look for the X shape \u2014 opposite angles in an X are always equal. V-O-E: Vertically Opposite = Equal."},{id:"fc05",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Two lines intersect. One of the four angles is 130\xb0. Find all four angles.",back:"Given: one angle = 130\xb0. Vertically opposite angle = 130\xb0 (equal). Linear pair angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 130\xb0 = 50\xb0. The other vertically opposite angle = 50\xb0. All four angles: 130\xb0, 50\xb0, 130\xb0, 50\xb0. Check: 130 + 50 + 130 + 50 = 360\xb0 \u2713 (angles at a point sum to 360\xb0).",memory_hook:"Two lines crossing = X shape = two pairs: equal opposites. Find one angle \u2192 opposite equals it \u2192 linear pair = 180 minus it \u2192 its opposite = it too. Always check they sum to 360\xb0."},{id:"fc06",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a transversal? How many angles does it create when crossing two lines?",back:"A transversal is a line that intersects two or more other lines at distinct (different) points. When a transversal crosses TWO lines: it creates 8 angles \u2014 4 at each intersection point. These 8 angles form special pairs: corresponding (4 pairs), alternate interior (2 pairs), alternate exterior (2 pairs), and co-interior/same-side interior (2 pairs). When the two lines are parallel, these angle pairs have specific equal or supplementary relationships.",memory_hook:"TRANSVERSAL = crosses lines. 2 lines crossed = 8 angles. Think of a road crossing two parallel railway tracks \u2014 the road creates 4 angles at each crossing = 8 total."},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are corresponding angles? Are they equal? What shape do they form?",back:"Corresponding angles are the angles in the same position at each intersection when a transversal crosses two lines \u2014 for example, both are above-left of their intersection. Property: When the two lines are parallel, corresponding angles are EQUAL. Shape: They form an F-shape (or its mirror image). Example: if a transversal crosses two parallel lines and one angle is 65\xb0, the corresponding angle at the other intersection is also 65\xb0.",memory_hook:"CORRESPONDING = same position = equal (for parallel lines). F-shape: draw the letter F \u2014 the two horizontal arms show the corresponding angles. F = Friendly (they're equal when lines are parallel)."},{id:"fc08",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are alternate interior angles? Are they equal? What shape do they form?",back:"Alternate interior angles are angles between the two lines (interior), on opposite sides (alternate) of the transversal. Property: When the two lines are parallel, alternate interior angles are EQUAL. Shape: They form a Z-shape or N-shape. Example: if a transversal crosses two parallel lines and one alternate interior angle is 55\xb0, the other alternate interior angle is also 55\xb0.",memory_hook:"ALTERNATE = ALTernate sides (opposite sides). INTERIOR = between the lines. Z-shape or N-shape. Z = zigzag between the lines to find the alternate interior angles. Equal when parallel."},{id:"fc09",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are co-interior angles? Are they equal? What is their relationship when lines are parallel?",back:"Co-interior angles (also called same-side interior angles or consecutive interior angles) are angles between the two lines (interior), on the SAME side of the transversal. Property: When the two lines are parallel, co-interior angles are SUPPLEMENTARY \u2014 they sum to 180\xb0. Shape: They form a C-shape or U-shape. Note: They are NOT equal (unless each is 90\xb0). Example: one co-interior angle = 70\xb0, the other = 110\xb0. Sum = 180\xb0.",memory_hook:"CO-INTERIOR = Co (together) + Interior (between lines) + Same side. C-shape. They're like partners adding up to 180\xb0. C = Complementary's bigger sibling (but C-interior = 180\xb0, not 90\xb0)."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the FZC rule for angles formed by a transversal crossing parallel lines.",back:"FZC is a memory rule for the three main angle relationships: F-shape \u2192 Corresponding angles \u2192 EQUAL. Z-shape (or N) \u2192 Alternate interior angles \u2192 EQUAL. C-shape (or U) \u2192 Co-interior angles \u2192 SUM TO 180\xb0 (supplementary). All three are only guaranteed when the lines are PARALLEL. Converse: if F/Z angles are equal, or C angles sum to 180\xb0, then the lines ARE parallel.",memory_hook:"FZC = Friendly Zebra Crossing. F (equal), Z (equal), C (180\xb0). Or: F and Z are friends (equal). C is different (sums to 180\xb0, not equal)."},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A transversal crosses two parallel lines. One angle = 115\xb0. Find the corresponding, alternate interior, and co-interior angle on the same side.",back:"Given angle = 115\xb0. Corresponding angle = 115\xb0 (F-shape, equal). Alternate interior angle = 115\xb0 (Z-shape, equal). Co-interior angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 115\xb0 = 65\xb0 (C-shape, supplementary). Check: 115\xb0 + 65\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Corresponding and alternate interior both MATCH the given angle (equal). Co-interior is the DIFFERENCE from 180\xb0. Two equals, one complement-to-180. FZC pattern every time."},{id:"fc12",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find the complement of 67\xb0 and the supplement of 123\xb0.",back:"Complement of 67\xb0 = 90\xb0 \u2212 67\xb0 = 23\xb0. Supplement of 123\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2212 123\xb0 = 57\xb0. Quick check: does a complement make sense? 67\xb0 < 90\xb0, so yes (angles > 90\xb0 have no complement). Does a supplement make sense? 123\xb0 < 180\xb0, so yes.",memory_hook:"Complement \u2192 subtract from 90. Supplement \u2192 subtract from 180. Always check the original angle is less than 90\xb0 (for complement) or less than 180\xb0 (for supplement)."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Two supplementary angles are in the ratio 4:5. Find both angles.",back:"Let the angles be 4x and 5x. Supplementary means: 4x + 5x = 180\xb0. 9x = 180\xb0. x = 20\xb0. Angles: 4 \xd7 20\xb0 = 80\xb0 and 5 \xd7 20\xb0 = 100\xb0. Check: 80\xb0 + 100\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Ratio problems: assign 'x' to each part. Set up the sum equation (90 for complementary, 180 for supplementary). Solve for x, then multiply. Always verify by checking the sum."},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Prove that vertically opposite angles are equal using the linear pair axiom.",back:"Proof: Let two lines intersect at O, forming angles \u22201, \u22202, \u22203, \u22204 in order. \u22201 and \u22202 form a linear pair \u2192 \u22201 + \u22202 = 180\xb0 ... (i). \u22202 and \u22203 form a linear pair \u2192 \u22202 + \u22203 = 180\xb0 ... (ii). From (i) and (ii): \u22201 + \u22202 = \u22202 + \u22203. Subtracting \u22202 from both sides: \u22201 = \u22203. Similarly, \u22202 = \u22204. Therefore vertically opposite angles are equal. QED.",memory_hook:"Proof strategy: Both angles share a linear pair with the SAME angle. If A+B=180 and B+C=180, then A=C. The shared middle angle cancels out."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Two lines are cut by a transversal. If \u22201 = 3x + 10\xb0 and its corresponding angle \u22202 = 5x \u2212 30\xb0, find x and both angles. Are the lines parallel?",back:"Corresponding angles are equal when lines are parallel. Set \u22201 = \u22202: 3x + 10 = 5x \u2212 30. 10 + 30 = 5x \u2212 3x. 40 = 2x. x = 20\xb0. \u22201 = 3(20) + 10 = 70\xb0. \u22202 = 5(20) \u2212 30 = 70\xb0. Since corresponding angles are equal (70\xb0 = 70\xb0), the lines ARE parallel.",memory_hook:"Corresponding angles equal \u2192 lines parallel. Set them equal, solve for x. Then check: does it make sense? Both angles must be the same."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between adjacent angles and a linear pair?",back:"Adjacent angles: share a common vertex AND a common arm, with their interiors not overlapping. They can have ANY sum (not necessarily 180\xb0). Linear pair: a SPECIAL case of adjacent angles where the two non-common arms form a straight line \u2014 so they must sum to 180\xb0. Key: all linear pairs are adjacent, but NOT all adjacent angles are linear pairs. Example: Two adjacent angles of 60\xb0 and 80\xb0 are adjacent but NOT a linear pair (60+80=140\u2260180).",memory_hook:"Adjacent = neighbours (share a side). Linear pair = neighbours on a STRAIGHT road (must sum to 180\xb0). Not all neighbours live on a straight road."},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In the figure, two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle is 73\xb0. Find the co-interior angle on the same side.",back:"Co-interior angles (C-shape) are supplementary when lines are parallel: co-interior angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 73\xb0 = 107\xb0. Check: 73\xb0 + 107\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2713. These two angles are between the parallel lines on the same side of the transversal \u2014 the C-shape confirms they must sum to 180\xb0.",memory_hook:"Co-interior \u2192 C-shape \u2192 sum = 180\xb0 \u2192 subtract given angle from 180\xb0. Simple subtraction. Co-interior is always the 'other half' of 180\xb0."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Angles at a point: five rays from a single point create five angles. Four of them are 45\xb0, 60\xb0, 85\xb0, and 95\xb0. Find the fifth angle.",back:"Angles around a complete point sum to 360\xb0. Sum of four known angles = 45 + 60 + 85 + 95 = 285\xb0. Fifth angle = 360\xb0 \u2212 285\xb0 = 75\xb0. Check: 45 + 60 + 85 + 95 + 75 = 360\xb0 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Full rotation = 360\xb0. All angles at a point sum to 360\xb0. Missing angle = 360\xb0 minus the sum of all known angles. Like completing a pie chart to 100%."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Karnataka's railway tracks run parallel. A road crosses them at 65\xb0 to one track. Find the alternate interior angle and the co-interior angle formed with the second track.",back:"The road is a transversal crossing two parallel railway tracks. Given angle = 65\xb0. Alternate interior angle (Z-shape) = 65\xb0 (equal, parallel lines). Co-interior angle (C-shape) = 180\xb0 \u2212 65\xb0 = 115\xb0 (supplementary, parallel lines). This models a real situation: if a road crosses railway tracks at 65\xb0, the angle on the other side of the road between the tracks is 65\xb0 (alternate), and the angle on the same side is 115\xb0 (co-interior).",memory_hook:"Real-world transversal problem: road = transversal, parallel tracks = parallel lines. Z = 65\xb0 (same as given). C = 180\xb0 \u2212 65\xb0 = 115\xb0."},{id:"fc20",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"How do you use angle relationships to PROVE two lines are parallel?",back:"To prove lines are parallel using angle evidence: (1) If corresponding angles are EQUAL \u2192 lines are parallel. (2) If alternate interior angles are EQUAL \u2192 lines are parallel. (3) If co-interior angles SUM TO 180\xb0 \u2192 lines are parallel. These are the CONVERSES of the parallel line properties. Example: two lines cut by a transversal give co-interior angles of 112\xb0 and 68\xb0. Sum = 112 + 68 = 180\xb0. Therefore the lines ARE parallel. This converse reasoning is used constantly in geometry proofs.",memory_hook:"To PROVE parallel: check FZC. F equal \u2192 parallel. Z equal \u2192 parallel. C sums to 180\xb0 \u2192 parallel. Evidence of the pattern \u2192 conclusion of parallel lines. Converse logic."}]}},2958,[]);